    <h2 id="section-2" style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: 30px;">2. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
    <div class="faq-container">
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h4 class="faq-question">Q1: Can a property be bought legally through a General Power of Attorney (GPA)?</h4>
            <p class="faq-answer">No. Following the Supreme Court ruling in <em>Suraj Lamp & Industries vs. State of Haryana</em>, a GPA transaction does not convey legal title or ownership. Legal title passes only via a registered Sale Deed executed at the SRO.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h4 class="faq-question">Q2: Does a Power of Attorney remain valid after the death of the principal?</h4>
            <p class="faq-answer">No. A Power of Attorney automatically terminates upon the death of the principal under Section 201 of the Indian Contract Act. Any transaction executed post-death by the attorney holder is legally void.</p>
